# Target C-II - same-oriented parallel three-cut triples admit an exact far-shore release

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only exact source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** the same-oriented projectively-parallel case in `target_c_three_cut_parallel_integration_classification_2026-09-15.md`  
**Dependencies:** exact finite laminar multishear; nested shore cutsets; canonical contact-minimal/global-minimal normalization  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The rank-one three-cut classification correctly proves that, if all three selected train
families are required to remain unit contacts, a same-oriented parallel triple has no
nonconstant exact full-support cancellation curve. That is not the final finite-source
obstruction in the canonical contact-minimal normalization.

One may preserve the first two train families exactly and deliberately open the third.
A compensating translation of the far shore then fixes both outermost layers exactly.
Consequently every diameter row meeting none or all three selected cuts is unchanged
**exactly**, while only the same bounded two-interface exceptional set can move.

Thus the last rank-one integration regression also reduces to an exact finite diameter
cusp. The price is favorable: one old unit-contact family is removed.

---

## 1. Three nested cuts with one common shore-oriented direction

Let

\[
\tau_1,\tau_2,\tau_3
\]

be three consecutive selected O--O cuts on one laminar complement-dual path. Let their
nested far shores be

\[
S_3\subset S_2\subset S_1,
\]

with indicators `chi_1,chi_2,chi_3`.

Assume the surviving same-orientation rank-one case from the parent classification:

\[
\boxed{u_1=u_2=u_3=u,\qquad |u|=1.}
\tag{FS.1}
\]

(The common negative orientation is absorbed by replacing `u` with `-u`.)

For angles `alpha,beta`, put

\[
\Delta_\alpha:=R_\alpha u-u,
\qquad
\Delta_\beta:=R_\beta u-u.
\tag{FS.2}

The usual exact cut shears on `tau_1,tau_2` would displace a source vertex `x` by

\[
\chi_1(x)\Delta_\alpha+\chi_2(x)\Delta_\beta.
\]

Now add one ordinary translation of the entire far shore `S_3` by

\[
-(\Delta_\alpha+\Delta_\beta).
\]

The complete finite displacement field is therefore

\[
\boxed{
W_{\alpha,\beta}(x)
=\chi_1(x)\Delta_\alpha
+\chi_2(x)\Delta_\beta
-\chi_3(x)(\Delta_\alpha+\Delta_\beta).
}
\tag{FS.3}

This is an actual finite source motion, not merely an infinitesimal velocity field.

The four consecutive layer displacements are

\[
\boxed{
0,\quad
\Delta_\alpha,\quad
\Delta_\alpha+\Delta_\beta,\quad
0.
}
\tag{FS.4}

In particular the two outermost layers are fixed relative to one another.

---

## 2. The first two train families remain unit and the third opens strictly

The audited shore-cut theorem says that the old unit contacts crossing `S_j` are exactly
the train contacts of `tau_j`. A contact outside the three selected train families has
the same `chi_j` value at both endpoints for every `j`, so it is translated rigidly and
is unchanged exactly.

A `tau_1` contact joins the first two layers in (FS.4). Its old vector is `u`, and its
new vector is

\[
u+\Delta_\alpha=R_\alpha u,
\]

so its length stays exactly one.

A `tau_2` contact joins the middle two layers. Its new vector is

\[
u+\Delta_\beta=R_\beta u,
\]

so its length also stays exactly one.

A `tau_3` contact joins the third and fourth layers. Its new vector is

\[
\boxed{
q(\alpha,\beta)
:=u-(\Delta_\alpha+\Delta_\beta)
=3u-R_\alpha u-R_\beta u.
}
\tag{FS.5}

Its squared-length excess is the exact identity

\[
\begin{aligned}
|q(\alpha,\beta)|^2-1
&=10-6\cos\alpha-6\cos\beta+2\cos(\alpha-\beta)\\
&=12\sin^2{\alpha\over2}
 +12\sin^2{\beta\over2}
 -4\sin^2{\alpha-\beta\over2}.
\end{aligned}
\tag{FS.6}

For real `A,B`,

\[
\sin^2(A-B)
\le2\bigl(\sin^2A+\sin^2B\bigr).
\]

Applying this with `A=alpha/2`, `B=beta/2` gives

\[
\boxed{
|q(\alpha,\beta)|^2-1
\ge4\left(
\sin^2{\alpha\over2}
+\sin^2{\beta\over2}
\right).
}
\tag{FS.7}

Hence in a neighborhood of `(0,0)`, every nonzero parameter pair satisfies

\[
\boxed{|q(\alpha,\beta)|>1.}
\tag{FS.8}

Thus `tau_3` is not merely allowed to move: every one of its old unit contacts opens
strictly under every nontrivial sufficiently small motion (FS.3).

---

## 3. Diameter rows meeting none or all three cuts are fixed exactly

Orient a source pair by increasing layer index. Its selected-cut support intersects
`{1,2,3}` in one contiguous subset. Under (FS.3), the exact relative-displacement
increment depends only on that subset:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
I_h\cap\{1,2,3\}&\text{relative increment}\\ \hline
\varnothing&0\\
\{1\}&\Delta_\alpha\\
\{2\}&\Delta_\beta\\
\{3\}&-(\Delta_\alpha+\Delta_\beta)\\
\{1,2\}&\Delta_\alpha+\Delta_\beta\\
\{2,3\}&-\Delta_\alpha\\
\{1,2,3\}&0.
\end{array}
\tag{FS.9}

The table follows immediately by subtracting the four layer displacements in (FS.4).
In particular

\[
\boxed{
I_h\cap\{1,2,3\}\in\{\varnothing,\{1,2,3\}\}
\Longrightarrow
r_h(\alpha,\beta)=r_h
\text{ exactly}.}
\tag{FS.10}

Every remaining support type changes incidence at one of the two internal boundaries.
Therefore the complete moving diameter set is the same two-interface exceptional set
from the three-cut kernel theorem:

\[
\boxed{
H_{mov}\subseteq H_{sw,1}\cup H_{sw,2}=H_{exc}.
}
\tag{FS.11}

No full-span quadratic drift remains. It has been cancelled by the far-shore
translation.

---

## 4. Canonical minimality converts any feasible parameter into a contradiction

Fix one finite canonical source. Finiteness gives a neighborhood `U` of `(0,0)` in
which every old strict lower-distance noncontact remains strictly longer than one and
every old strict subdiameter pair remains strictly shorter than `D` under (FS.3).
Suppose there is a nonzero pair

\[
(alpha,beta)\in U
\]

for which every moving actual diameter in `H_exc` remains at most `D`.

By Sections 2--3:

- every old unit contact outside `tau_3` remains exactly unit;
- every old `tau_3` contact becomes strictly longer than one;
- every old diameter outside `H_exc` is unchanged exactly;
- every moving old diameter is at most `D` by assumption.

All remaining old source pairs were strict lower-distance noncontacts and/or strict
subdiameter pairs. Their strict slacks persist because `(alpha,beta)` was chosen in
`U`. No new unit contact is created.

The resulting source is therefore unit-separated, has the same cardinality, and has
diameter at most `D`, but has strictly fewer unit contacts.

- If its diameter is below `D`, global minimality is contradicted.
- If its diameter equals `D`, the canonical contact-minimal choice is contradicted.

Consequently a survivor must satisfy the exact finite infeasibility statement

\[
\boxed{
\text{every sufficiently small nonzero }(\alpha,\beta)
\text{ makes at least one }h\in H_{exc}
\text{ exceed }D.
}
\tag{FS.12}

Thus the same-oriented rank-one branch is not a global second-order integration
obstruction. It is another finite exceptional-diameter cusp.

---

## 5. First-order normal form on the two parameter coordinates

Although the finite theorem above is exact, its tangent system is especially simple.
At `(alpha,beta)=(0,0)`,

\[
\Delta_\alpha=\alpha Ju+O(\alpha^2),
\qquad
\Delta_\beta=\beta Ju+O(\beta^2).
\]

For one diameter vector `r_h`, put

\[
\eta_h:=r_h\cdot Ju.
\]

Up to the common factor `2`, the first squared-length derivative is one of only three
linear forms:

\[
\begin{array}{c|c}
I_h\cap\{1,2,3\}&\text{first-order form}/(2\eta_h)\\ \hline
\{1\}&\alpha\\
\{2,3\}&-\alpha\\
\{2\}&\beta\\
\{3\}&-(\alpha+\beta)\\
\{1,2\}&\alpha+\beta.
\end{array}
\tag{FS.13}

Thus the entire same-oriented exceptional cusp lives on the three projective parameter
rays

\[
\boxed{
\alpha,\qquad \beta,\qquad \alpha+\beta.
}
\tag{FS.14}

The next finite classification can therefore combine the two audited one-arc switching
interfaces with a three-ray planar tangent system. It need not handle an arbitrary
family of first-order functionals.

---

## 6. Relation to the parent rank-one classification

The parent theorem proves the exact statement

\[
G(\theta_1,\theta_2,\theta_3)=0
\Longrightarrow \theta=0
\]

near the origin when the three shore-oriented train vectors are equal and **all three
train families are kept unit by train rotations**. The radial defect there is genuine.

The present theorem does not contradict that calculation. It changes the finite move:

1. rotate only the first two train cuts;
2. translate the entire far shore to cancel their accumulated displacement;
3. deliberately open the third train family.

The canonical optimization makes step 3 useful rather than forbidden. The result is
an exact localization theorem unavailable inside the all-contacts-preserved chart.

Combining this with the parent classification yields:

```text
rank two triple:
    exact full-support cancellation curve;

rank one, mixed shore signs:
    exact pair-cancellation curve;

rank one, same shore sign:
    exact far-shore-corrected finite release,
    preserving two selected train families and opening the third.
```

Hence **every** three-cut orientation class now admits a genuine finite source motion
for which all diameter rows outside the two switching interfaces are unchanged exactly.
Only the bounded positive-KKT exceptional cusp remains.

No diameter descent or C-II closure is claimed here.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only exact far-shore release for the last same-oriented
parallel three-cut integration regression. The three-cut finite localization is now
complete across all orientation classes; C-II and Level I remain open.
